{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rate limiting allows a configured number of executions within a time window. Calls run immediately while capacity remains. Once the limit is reached, later calls are rejected until capacity becomes available again."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Pacer provides an in-memory rate limiter intended primarily for client-side operations. It can run in server-side JavaScript, but it is not a distributed quota or enforcement system."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-rate-limiting-works","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How rate limiting works"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This example allows three executions per window:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"Rate Limiting (limit: 3 calls per window)\nTimeline: [1 second per tick]\n                                        Window 1                  | Window 2\nCalls:        ⬇️     ⬇️     ⬇️     ⬇️     ⬇️                       ⬇️     ⬇️\nExecuted:     ✅     ✅     ✅     ❌     ❌                       ✅     ✅\n             [=== 3 allowed ===][=== blocked until reset ===][=== new window ===]","lang":"text"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rate limiting permits bursts. It does not space accepted calls evenly."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"when-to-use-rate-limiting","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When to use rate limiting"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose rate limiting when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A client operation should follow a fixed quota."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Calls may run immediately until the quota is exhausted."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rejected calls may be discarded or handled separately."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You need to measure remaining capacity or time until capacity returns."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose another utility when:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Executions should be evenly spaced. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./throttling.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"throttling"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Only the final call matters. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./debouncing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"debouncing"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every operation must eventually run. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./queuing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"queuing"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Items should run together. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./batching.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"batching"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You need Promise results, retries, or abort support. Use "},{"type":"link","href":"./async-rate-limiting.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"async rate limiting"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"window-types","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Window types"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"windowType"},{"type":"text","value":" option controls when capacity returns."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"fixed-window","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed window"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A fixed window starts when its first execution is accepted. All accepted executions remain counted until that window ends. Capacity then resets together."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const limiter = createRateLimiter(sendEvent, {\n  limit: 3,\n  window: 1000,\n  windowType: 'fixed',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Fixed windows can allow bursts near a boundary because a full quota becomes available when the window resets."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"sliding-window","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sliding window"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A sliding window tracks each accepted execution separately. Capacity returns one execution at a time as old timestamps leave the window."}]},{"type":"code","value":"Sliding Window (limit: 3 calls per window)\nTimeline: [1 second per tick]\nCalls:        ⬇️     ⬇️     ⬇️     ⬇️           ⬇️\nExecuted:     ✅     ✅     ✅     ❌           ✅\n             [=== full ===][oldest execution expires][=== one available ===]","lang":"text"},{"type":"code","value":"const limiter = createRateLimiter(sendEvent, {\n  limit: 3,\n  window: 1000,\n  windowType: 'sliding',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a sliding window when capacity should return gradually rather than all at once."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-an-api","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose an API"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createRateLimitedSignal"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createRateLimitedValue"},{"type":"text","value":" for Svelte state"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createRateLimiter"},{"type":"text","value":" for capacity helpers and selected state"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"maybeExecute()"},{"type":"text","value":" for quota-controlled operations. Use state or value helpers for UI updates, and the instance for capacity helpers and rejection state."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"svelte-example","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Svelte example"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Create the utility during component initialization. Destroying the component runs its cleanup. The example imports application operations from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"./api"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createRateLimiter } from '@tanstack/svelte-pacer'\n  import { sendEvent } from './api'\n\n  const limiter = createRateLimiter(\n    sendEvent,\n    { limit: 3, window: 10_000 },\n    (state) => ({\n      rejectionCount: state.rejectionCount,\n      executionCount: state.executionCount,\n    }),\n  )\n</script>\n\n<button onclick={() => void limiter.maybeExecute('clicked')}>Send</button>\n<output>{limiter.state.rejectionCount}</output>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The focused snippets below use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createRateLimiter"},{"type":"text","value":" during component initialization, and instance methods from event handlers."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"rate-limited-callback","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rate-limited callback"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createRateLimiter(...).maybeExecute"},{"type":"text","value":" when an event should invoke a rate-limited side effect:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createRateLimiter } from '@tanstack/svelte-pacer'\n\nconst search = createRateLimiter(\n  (query: string) => updateSearchResults(query),\n  { limit: 3, window: 1000 },\n).maybeExecute\nfunction onInput(event: Event) {\n  search((event.target as HTMLInputElement).value)\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The callback returns whether the call was accepted. It does not expose capacity helpers or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reset()"},{"type":"text","value":". Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createRateLimiter"},{"type":"text","value":" when the component needs that control."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"rate-limited-state-and-values","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rate-limited state and values"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createRateLimitedSignal"},{"type":"text","value":" when Pacer should own the rate-limited state, or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createRateLimitedValue"},{"type":"text","value":" when a value already changes elsewhere:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createRateLimitedValue } from '@tanstack/svelte-pacer'\n\nlet query = $state('')\nconst [rateLimitedQuery] = createRateLimitedValue(() => query, {\n  limit: 3,\n  window: 1000,\n})\n// Read rateLimitedQuery() in the template.","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"handling-rejected-calls","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handling rejected calls"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rejected calls do not run later. Use the boolean return value or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onReject"},{"type":"text","value":" to provide feedback, retry elsewhere, or place work into a queue."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const limiter = createRateLimiter(sendEvent, {\n  limit: 2,\n  window: 1000,\n  onReject: (limiter) => {\n    console.log('Rejected calls:', limiter.store.state.rejectionCount)\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If rejected operations must eventually run, a "},{"type":"link","href":"./queuing.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"queuer"}]},{"type":"text","value":" is usually a better fit."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"inspecting-capacity","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inspecting capacity"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The instance API provides two computed helpers:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"limiter.getRemainingInWindow() // Accepted executions still available.\nlimiter.getMsUntilNextWindow() // Time until at least one execution is available.","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both helpers use the current "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"limit"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"window"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"windowType"},{"type":"text","value":", and execution history."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"resetting-and-configuring-the-limiter","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resetting and configuring the limiter"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" clears execution timestamps, counters, and cleanup timers. The next call starts with full capacity."}]},{"type":"code","value":"limiter.reset()","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setOptions()"},{"type":"text","value":" to update the configuration:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"limiter.setOptions({\n  limit: 10,\n  window: 30_000,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Changing options does not erase existing execution history. Call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"reset()"},{"type":"text","value":" when the new configuration should begin with a fresh window."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"limit"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"window"},{"type":"text","value":" options may be functions that receive the limiter instance:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const limiter = createRateLimiter(sendEvent, {\n  enabled: (limiter) => limiter.store.state.executionCount < 100,\n  limit: (limiter) => (limiter.store.state.rejectionCount > 10 ? 2 : 5),\n  window: 60_000,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disabling the limiter prevents the wrapped function from executing. It does not delete existing execution history."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"observing-executions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Observing executions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onExecute"},{"type":"text","value":" receives the executed arguments and limiter instance. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onReject"},{"type":"text","value":" receives the limiter instance."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const limiter = createRateLimiter(sendEvent, {\n  limit: 5,\n  window: 1000,\n  onExecute: (args, limiter) => {\n    console.log('Sent:', args)\n    console.log('Remaining:', limiter.getRemainingInWindow())\n  },\n  onReject: (limiter) => {\n    console.log('Rejected:', limiter.store.state.rejectionCount)\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"svelte-lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Svelte lifecycle"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter has no default operation cleanup because a synchronous limiter has no pending or active work. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"onUnmount"},{"type":"text","value":" only when the component needs custom teardown related to the limiter."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reactive-state","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reactive state"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The adapter subscribes only to the state returned by the selector argument. Without a selector, the adapter state is empty. Read selected state from the utility in the template. Imperative methods such as capacity and peek helpers do not create reactive dependencies; derive their readouts from selected state:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const limiter = createRateLimiter(\n  sendEvent,\n  { limit: 5, window: 60_000 },\n  (state) => ({\n    isExceeded: state.isExceeded,\n    rejectionCount: state.rejectionCount,\n  }),\n)\n\nconsole.log(limiter.state.isExceeded, limiter.state.rejectionCount)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"utility.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" with a selector and a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"children"},{"type":"text","value":" snippet for an independently subscribed child."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Option functions and lifecycle callbacks receive the underlying public utility instance. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":".store.state"},{"type":"text","value":" reads inside those callbacks in the examples above are supported. Rendering code should read the selected adapter state shown here."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To restore selected state that your app has persisted, pass a partial snapshot through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"initialState"},{"type":"text","value":". It is merged with the defaults. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers are not restored."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Commonly useful state includes:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"executionCount"},{"type":"text","value":": Total accepted executions that completed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"executionTimes"},{"type":"text","value":": Timestamps currently used for window calculations."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"isExceeded"},{"type":"text","value":": Whether the current limit has been reached."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"rejectionCount"},{"type":"text","value":": Calls rejected because the window was full."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"status"},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'disabled'"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'exceeded'"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'idle'"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/index.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Svelte API reference"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for adapter signatures and the public core reference for complete option and state types."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"how-rate-limiting-works","text":"How rate limiting works","level":2},{"id":"when-to-use-rate-limiting","text":"When to use rate limiting","level":2},{"id":"window-types","text":"Window types","level":2},{"id":"fixed-window","text":"Fixed window","level":3},{"id":"sliding-window","text":"Sliding window","level":3},{"id":"choose-an-api","text":"Choose an API","level":2},{"id":"svelte-example","text":"Svelte example","level":2},{"id":"rate-limited-callback","text":"Rate-limited callback","level":3},{"id":"rate-limited-state-and-values","text":"Rate-limited state and values","level":3},{"id":"handling-rejected-calls","text":"Handling rejected calls","level":2},{"id":"inspecting-capacity","text":"Inspecting capacity","level":2},{"id":"resetting-and-configuring-the-limiter","text":"Resetting and configuring the limiter","level":2},{"id":"observing-executions","text":"Observing executions","level":3},{"id":"svelte-lifecycle","text":"Svelte lifecycle","level":2},{"id":"reactive-state","text":"Reactive state","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nRate limiting allows a configured number of executions within a time window. Calls run immediately while capacity remains. Once the limit is reached, later calls are rejected until capacity becomes available again.\n\nTanStack Pacer provides an in-memory rate limiter intended primarily for client-side operations. It can run in server-side JavaScript, but it is not a distributed quota or enforcement system.\n\n## How rate limiting works\n\nThis example allows three executions per window:\n\n```text\nRate Limiting (limit: 3 calls per window)\nTimeline: [1 second per tick]\n                                        Window 1                  | Window 2\nCalls:        ⬇️     ⬇️     ⬇️     ⬇️     ⬇️                       ⬇️     ⬇️\nExecuted:     ✅     ✅     ✅     ❌     ❌                       ✅     ✅\n             [=== 3 allowed ===][=== blocked until reset ===][=== new window ===]\n```\n\nRate limiting permits bursts. It does not space accepted calls evenly.\n\n## When to use rate limiting\n\nChoose rate limiting when:\n\n- A client operation should follow a fixed quota.\n- Calls may run immediately until the quota is exhausted.\n- Rejected calls may be discarded or handled separately.\n- You need to measure remaining capacity or time until capacity returns.\n\nChoose another utility when:\n\n- Executions should be evenly spaced. Use [throttling](./throttling.md).\n- Only the final call matters. Use [debouncing](./debouncing.md).\n- Every operation must eventually run. Use [queuing](./queuing.md).\n- Items should run together. Use [batching](./batching.md).\n- You need Promise results, retries, or abort support. Use [async rate limiting](./async-rate-limiting.md).\n\n## Window types\n\nThe `windowType` option controls when capacity returns.\n\n### Fixed window\n\nA fixed window starts when its first execution is accepted. All accepted executions remain counted until that window ends. Capacity then resets together.\n\n```ts\nconst limiter = createRateLimiter(sendEvent, {\n  limit: 3,\n  window: 1000,\n  windowType: 'fixed',\n})\n```\n\nFixed windows can allow bursts near a boundary because a full quota becomes available when the window resets.\n\n### Sliding window\n\nA sliding window tracks each accepted execution separately. Capacity returns one execution at a time as old timestamps leave the window.\n\n```text\nSliding Window (limit: 3 calls per window)\nTimeline: [1 second per tick]\nCalls:        ⬇️     ⬇️     ⬇️     ⬇️           ⬇️\nExecuted:     ✅     ✅     ✅     ❌           ✅\n             [=== full ===][oldest execution expires][=== one available ===]\n```\n\n```ts\nconst limiter = createRateLimiter(sendEvent, {\n  limit: 3,\n  window: 1000,\n  windowType: 'sliding',\n})\n```\n\nUse a sliding window when capacity should return gradually rather than all at once.\n\n## Choose an API\n\n- `createRateLimitedSignal` or `createRateLimitedValue` for Svelte state\n- `createRateLimiter` for capacity helpers and selected state\n\nCall `maybeExecute()` for quota-controlled operations. Use state or value helpers for UI updates, and the instance for capacity helpers and rejection state.\n\n## Svelte example\n\nCreate the utility during component initialization. Destroying the component runs its cleanup. The example imports application operations from `./api`.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createRateLimiter } from '@tanstack/svelte-pacer'\n  import { sendEvent } from './api'\n\n  const limiter = createRateLimiter(\n    sendEvent,\n    { limit: 3, window: 10_000 },\n    (state) => ({\n      rejectionCount: state.rejectionCount,\n      executionCount: state.executionCount,\n    }),\n  )\n</script>\n\n<button onclick={() => void limiter.maybeExecute('clicked')}>Send</button>\n<output>{limiter.state.rejectionCount}</output>\n```\n\nThe focused snippets below use `createRateLimiter` during component initialization, and instance methods from event handlers.\n\n### Rate-limited callback\n\nUse `createRateLimiter(...).maybeExecute` when an event should invoke a rate-limited side effect:\n\n```ts\nimport { createRateLimiter } from '@tanstack/svelte-pacer'\n\nconst search = createRateLimiter(\n  (query: string) => updateSearchResults(query),\n  { limit: 3, window: 1000 },\n).maybeExecute\nfunction onInput(event: Event) {\n  search((event.target as HTMLInputElement).value)\n}\n```\n\nThe callback returns whether the call was accepted. It does not expose capacity helpers or `reset()`. Use `createRateLimiter` when the component needs that control.\n\n### Rate-limited state and values\n\nUse `createRateLimitedSignal` when Pacer should own the rate-limited state, or `createRateLimitedValue` when a value already changes elsewhere:\n\n```ts\nimport { createRateLimitedValue } from '@tanstack/svelte-pacer'\n\nlet query = $state('')\nconst [rateLimitedQuery] = createRateLimitedValue(() => query, {\n  limit: 3,\n  window: 1000,\n})\n// Read rateLimitedQuery() in the template.\n```\n\n## Handling rejected calls\n\nRejected calls do not run later. Use the boolean return value or `onReject` to provide feedback, retry elsewhere, or place work into a queue.\n\n```ts\nconst limiter = createRateLimiter(sendEvent, {\n  limit: 2,\n  window: 1000,\n  onReject: (limiter) => {\n    console.log('Rejected calls:', limiter.store.state.rejectionCount)\n  },\n})\n```\n\nIf rejected operations must eventually run, a [queuer](./queuing.md) is usually a better fit.\n\n## Inspecting capacity\n\nThe instance API provides two computed helpers:\n\n```ts\nlimiter.getRemainingInWindow() // Accepted executions still available.\nlimiter.getMsUntilNextWindow() // Time until at least one execution is available.\n```\n\nBoth helpers use the current `limit`, `window`, `windowType`, and execution history.\n\n## Resetting and configuring the limiter\n\n`reset()` clears execution timestamps, counters, and cleanup timers. The next call starts with full capacity.\n\n```ts\nlimiter.reset()\n```\n\nUse `setOptions()` to update the configuration:\n\n```ts\nlimiter.setOptions({\n  limit: 10,\n  window: 30_000,\n})\n```\n\nChanging options does not erase existing execution history. Call `reset()` when the new configuration should begin with a fresh window.\n\nThe `enabled`, `limit`, and `window` options may be functions that receive the limiter instance:\n\n```ts\nconst limiter = createRateLimiter(sendEvent, {\n  enabled: (limiter) => limiter.store.state.executionCount < 100,\n  limit: (limiter) => (limiter.store.state.rejectionCount > 10 ? 2 : 5),\n  window: 60_000,\n})\n```\n\nDisabling the limiter prevents the wrapped function from executing. It does not delete existing execution history.\n\n### Observing executions\n\n`onExecute` receives the executed arguments and limiter instance. `onReject` receives the limiter instance.\n\n```ts\nconst limiter = createRateLimiter(sendEvent, {\n  limit: 5,\n  window: 1000,\n  onExecute: (args, limiter) => {\n    console.log('Sent:', args)\n    console.log('Remaining:', limiter.getRemainingInWindow())\n  },\n  onReject: (limiter) => {\n    console.log('Rejected:', limiter.store.state.rejectionCount)\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Svelte lifecycle\n\nThe adapter has no default operation cleanup because a synchronous limiter has no pending or active work. Use `onUnmount` only when the component needs custom teardown related to the limiter.\n\n## Reactive state\n\nThe adapter subscribes only to the state returned by the selector argument. Without a selector, the adapter state is empty. Read selected state from the utility in the template. Imperative methods such as capacity and peek helpers do not create reactive dependencies; derive their readouts from selected state:\n\n```ts\nconst limiter = createRateLimiter(\n  sendEvent,\n  { limit: 5, window: 60_000 },\n  (state) => ({\n    isExceeded: state.isExceeded,\n    rejectionCount: state.rejectionCount,\n  }),\n)\n\nconsole.log(limiter.state.isExceeded, limiter.state.rejectionCount)\n```\n\nUse `utility.Subscribe` with a selector and a `children` snippet for an independently subscribed child.\n\nOption functions and lifecycle callbacks receive the underlying public utility instance. The `.store.state` reads inside those callbacks in the examples above are supported. Rendering code should read the selected adapter state shown here.\n\nTo restore selected state that your app has persisted, pass a partial snapshot through `initialState`. It is merged with the defaults. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers are not restored.\n\nCommonly useful state includes:\n\n- `executionCount`: Total accepted executions that completed.\n- `executionTimes`: Timestamps currently used for window calculations.\n- `isExceeded`: Whether the current limit has been reached.\n- `rejectionCount`: Calls rejected because the window was full.\n- `status`: `'disabled'`, `'exceeded'`, or `'idle'`.\n\nSee the [Svelte API reference](../reference/index.md) for adapter signatures and the public core reference for complete option and state types.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Svelte Rate Limiting Guide\nid: rate-limiting\n---\n\nRate limiting allows a configured number of executions within a time window. Calls run immediately while capacity remains. Once the limit is reached, later calls are rejected until capacity becomes available again.\n\nTanStack Pacer provides an in-memory rate limiter intended primarily for client-side operations. It can run in server-side JavaScript, but it is not a distributed quota or enforcement system.\n\n## How rate limiting works\n\nThis example allows three executions per window:\n\n```text\nRate Limiting (limit: 3 calls per window)\nTimeline: [1 second per tick]\n                                        Window 1                  | Window 2\nCalls:        ⬇️     ⬇️     ⬇️     ⬇️     ⬇️                       ⬇️     ⬇️\nExecuted:     ✅     ✅     ✅     ❌     ❌                       ✅     ✅\n             [=== 3 allowed ===][=== blocked until reset ===][=== new window ===]\n```\n\nRate limiting permits bursts. It does not space accepted calls evenly.\n\n## When to use rate limiting\n\nChoose rate limiting when:\n\n- A client operation should follow a fixed quota.\n- Calls may run immediately until the quota is exhausted.\n- Rejected calls may be discarded or handled separately.\n- You need to measure remaining capacity or time until capacity returns.\n\nChoose another utility when:\n\n- Executions should be evenly spaced. Use [throttling](./throttling.md).\n- Only the final call matters. Use [debouncing](./debouncing.md).\n- Every operation must eventually run. Use [queuing](./queuing.md).\n- Items should run together. Use [batching](./batching.md).\n- You need Promise results, retries, or abort support. Use [async rate limiting](./async-rate-limiting.md).\n\n## Window types\n\nThe `windowType` option controls when capacity returns.\n\n### Fixed window\n\nA fixed window starts when its first execution is accepted. All accepted executions remain counted until that window ends. Capacity then resets together.\n\n```ts\nconst limiter = createRateLimiter(sendEvent, {\n  limit: 3,\n  window: 1000,\n  windowType: 'fixed',\n})\n```\n\nFixed windows can allow bursts near a boundary because a full quota becomes available when the window resets.\n\n### Sliding window\n\nA sliding window tracks each accepted execution separately. Capacity returns one execution at a time as old timestamps leave the window.\n\n```text\nSliding Window (limit: 3 calls per window)\nTimeline: [1 second per tick]\nCalls:        ⬇️     ⬇️     ⬇️     ⬇️           ⬇️\nExecuted:     ✅     ✅     ✅     ❌           ✅\n             [=== full ===][oldest execution expires][=== one available ===]\n```\n\n```ts\nconst limiter = createRateLimiter(sendEvent, {\n  limit: 3,\n  window: 1000,\n  windowType: 'sliding',\n})\n```\n\nUse a sliding window when capacity should return gradually rather than all at once.\n\n## Choose an API\n\n- `createRateLimitedSignal` or `createRateLimitedValue` for Svelte state\n- `createRateLimiter` for capacity helpers and selected state\n\nCall `maybeExecute()` for quota-controlled operations. Use state or value helpers for UI updates, and the instance for capacity helpers and rejection state.\n\n## Svelte example\n\nCreate the utility during component initialization. Destroying the component runs its cleanup. The example imports application operations from `./api`.\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\n  import { createRateLimiter } from '@tanstack/svelte-pacer'\n  import { sendEvent } from './api'\n\n  const limiter = createRateLimiter(\n    sendEvent,\n    { limit: 3, window: 10_000 },\n    (state) => ({\n      rejectionCount: state.rejectionCount,\n      executionCount: state.executionCount,\n    }),\n  )\n</script>\n\n<button onclick={() => void limiter.maybeExecute('clicked')}>Send</button>\n<output>{limiter.state.rejectionCount}</output>\n```\n\nThe focused snippets below use `createRateLimiter` during component initialization, and instance methods from event handlers.\n\n### Rate-limited callback\n\nUse `createRateLimiter(...).maybeExecute` when an event should invoke a rate-limited side effect:\n\n```ts\nimport { createRateLimiter } from '@tanstack/svelte-pacer'\n\nconst search = createRateLimiter(\n  (query: string) => updateSearchResults(query),\n  { limit: 3, window: 1000 },\n).maybeExecute\nfunction onInput(event: Event) {\n  search((event.target as HTMLInputElement).value)\n}\n```\n\nThe callback returns whether the call was accepted. It does not expose capacity helpers or `reset()`. Use `createRateLimiter` when the component needs that control.\n\n### Rate-limited state and values\n\nUse `createRateLimitedSignal` when Pacer should own the rate-limited state, or `createRateLimitedValue` when a value already changes elsewhere:\n\n```ts\nimport { createRateLimitedValue } from '@tanstack/svelte-pacer'\n\nlet query = $state('')\nconst [rateLimitedQuery] = createRateLimitedValue(() => query, {\n  limit: 3,\n  window: 1000,\n})\n// Read rateLimitedQuery() in the template.\n```\n\n## Handling rejected calls\n\nRejected calls do not run later. Use the boolean return value or `onReject` to provide feedback, retry elsewhere, or place work into a queue.\n\n```ts\nconst limiter = createRateLimiter(sendEvent, {\n  limit: 2,\n  window: 1000,\n  onReject: (limiter) => {\n    console.log('Rejected calls:', limiter.store.state.rejectionCount)\n  },\n})\n```\n\nIf rejected operations must eventually run, a [queuer](./queuing.md) is usually a better fit.\n\n## Inspecting capacity\n\nThe instance API provides two computed helpers:\n\n```ts\nlimiter.getRemainingInWindow() // Accepted executions still available.\nlimiter.getMsUntilNextWindow() // Time until at least one execution is available.\n```\n\nBoth helpers use the current `limit`, `window`, `windowType`, and execution history.\n\n## Resetting and configuring the limiter\n\n`reset()` clears execution timestamps, counters, and cleanup timers. The next call starts with full capacity.\n\n```ts\nlimiter.reset()\n```\n\nUse `setOptions()` to update the configuration:\n\n```ts\nlimiter.setOptions({\n  limit: 10,\n  window: 30_000,\n})\n```\n\nChanging options does not erase existing execution history. Call `reset()` when the new configuration should begin with a fresh window.\n\nThe `enabled`, `limit`, and `window` options may be functions that receive the limiter instance:\n\n```ts\nconst limiter = createRateLimiter(sendEvent, {\n  enabled: (limiter) => limiter.store.state.executionCount < 100,\n  limit: (limiter) => (limiter.store.state.rejectionCount > 10 ? 2 : 5),\n  window: 60_000,\n})\n```\n\nDisabling the limiter prevents the wrapped function from executing. It does not delete existing execution history.\n\n### Observing executions\n\n`onExecute` receives the executed arguments and limiter instance. `onReject` receives the limiter instance.\n\n```ts\nconst limiter = createRateLimiter(sendEvent, {\n  limit: 5,\n  window: 1000,\n  onExecute: (args, limiter) => {\n    console.log('Sent:', args)\n    console.log('Remaining:', limiter.getRemainingInWindow())\n  },\n  onReject: (limiter) => {\n    console.log('Rejected:', limiter.store.state.rejectionCount)\n  },\n})\n```\n\n## Svelte lifecycle\n\nThe adapter has no default operation cleanup because a synchronous limiter has no pending or active work. Use `onUnmount` only when the component needs custom teardown related to the limiter.\n\n## Reactive state\n\nThe adapter subscribes only to the state returned by the selector argument. Without a selector, the adapter state is empty. Read selected state from the utility in the template. Imperative methods such as capacity and peek helpers do not create reactive dependencies; derive their readouts from selected state:\n\n```ts\nconst limiter = createRateLimiter(\n  sendEvent,\n  { limit: 5, window: 60_000 },\n  (state) => ({\n    isExceeded: state.isExceeded,\n    rejectionCount: state.rejectionCount,\n  }),\n)\n\nconsole.log(limiter.state.isExceeded, limiter.state.rejectionCount)\n```\n\nUse `utility.Subscribe` with a selector and a `children` snippet for an independently subscribed child.\n\nOption functions and lifecycle callbacks receive the underlying public utility instance. The `.store.state` reads inside those callbacks in the examples above are supported. Rendering code should read the selected adapter state shown here.\n\nTo restore selected state that your app has persisted, pass a partial snapshot through `initialState`. It is merged with the defaults. Restore only durable fields. Pending timers are not restored.\n\nCommonly useful state includes:\n\n- `executionCount`: Total accepted executions that completed.\n- `executionTimes`: Timestamps currently used for window calculations.\n- `isExceeded`: Whether the current limit has been reached.\n- `rejectionCount`: Calls rejected because the window was full.\n- `status`: `'disabled'`, `'exceeded'`, or `'idle'`.\n\nSee the [Svelte API reference](../reference/index.md) for adapter signatures and the public core reference for complete option and state types.\n"}
